@anon.b8a8f256d6b0dabde04650efd080b696.553 = private unnamed_addr constant [1206 x i8] c" ## What it does\0A Checks for classes whose bases define incompatible `__slots__`.\0A\0A ## Why is this bad?\0A Inheriting from bases with incompatible `__slots__`s\0A will lead to a `TypeError` at runtime.\0A\0A Classes with no or empty `__slots__` are always compatible:\0A\0A ```python\0A class A: ...\0A class B:\0A     __slots__ = ()\0A class C:\0A     __slots__ = (\22a\22, \22b\22)\0A\0A # fine\0A class D(A, B, C): ...\0A ```\0A\0A Multiple inheritance from more than one different class\0A defining non-empty `__slots__` is not allowed:\0A\0A ```python\0A class A:\0A     __slots__ = (\22a\22, \22b\22)\0A\0A class B:\0A     __slots__ = (\22a\22, \22b\22)  # Even if the values are the same\0A\0A # TypeError: multiple bases have instance lay-out conflict\0A class C(A, B): ...\0A ```\0A\0A ## Known problems\0A Dynamic (not tuple or string literal) `__slots__` are not checked.\0A Additionally, classes inheriting from built-in classes with implicit layouts\0A like `str` or `int` are also not checked.\0A\0A ```pycon\0A >>> hasattr(int, \22__slots__\22)\0A False\0A >>> hasattr(str, \22__slots__\22)\0A False\0A >>> class A(int, str): ...\0A Traceback (most recent call last):\0A   File \22<python-input-0>\22, line 1, in <module>\0A     class A(int, str): ...\0A TypeError: multiple bases have instance lay-out conflict\0A ```\0A", align 1
