@anon.b8a8f256d6b0dabde04650efd080b696.607 = private unnamed_addr constant [898 x i8] c" ## What it does\0A Detects `super()` calls where:\0A - the first argument is not a valid class literal, or\0A - the second argument is not an instance or subclass of the first argument.\0A\0A ## Why is this bad?\0A `super(type, obj)` expects:\0A - the first argument to be a class,\0A - and the second argument to satisfy one of the following:\0A   - `isinstance(obj, type)` is `True`\0A   - `issubclass(obj, type)` is `True`\0A\0A Violating this relationship will raise a `TypeError` at runtime.\0A\0A ## Examples\0A ```python\0A class A:\0A     ...\0A class B(A):\0A     ...\0A\0A super(A, B())  # it's okay! `A` satisfies `isinstance(B(), A)`\0A\0A super(A(), B()) # error: `A()` is not a class\0A\0A super(B, A())  # error: `A()` does not satisfy `isinstance(A(), B)`\0A super(B, A)  # error: `A` does not satisfy `issubclass(A, B)`\0A ```\0A\0A ## References\0A - [Python documentation: super()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#super)\0A", align 1
