@anon.b8a8f256d6b0dabde04650efd080b696.610 = private unnamed_addr constant [691 x i8] c" ## What it does\0A Checks for a value other than `False` assigned to the `TYPE_CHECKING` variable, or an\0A annotation not assignable from `bool`.\0A\0A ## Why is this bad?\0A The name `TYPE_CHECKING` is reserved for a flag that can be used to provide conditional\0A code seen only by the type checker, and not at runtime. Normally this flag is imported from\0A `typing` or `typing_extensions`, but it can also be defined locally. If defined locally, it\0A must be assigned the value `False` at runtime; the type checker will consider its value to\0A be `True`. If annotated, it must be annotated as a type that can accept `bool` values.\0A\0A ## Examples\0A ```python\0A TYPE_CHECKING: str\0A TYPE_CHECKING = ''\0A ```\0A", align 1
